Overview
The bq2477x is a battery charge controller made by Texas Instruments. 
It manages charging batteries in devices like laptops, power banks, or embedded systems. 
It also monitors system power and can protect the processor from overheating. Think of it as the “smart manager” for your battery and system power.
1. Host-controlled NVDC-1 1S-4S Battery Charge Controller

1S-4S → Means it can charge 1 to 4 lithium-ion battery cells in series.

1S = 1 battery cell, 4S = 4 battery cells in series.

NVDC → Stands for Non-volatile Direct Current, meaning it controls DC battery charging while remembering settings even if power is lost.

Host-controlled → The main processor (CPU) can control charging settings through communication protocols.


2. Input Voltage Range: 4.5-24 V

The charger can take any voltage between 4.5V and 24V from an adapter or USB-C charger.

Flexible input makes it usable with many adapters.


3. Communication Protocols: SMBus & I2C

SMBus (System Management Bus) – A communication standard used mainly for battery management in PCs.

I2C (Inter-Integrated Circuit) – A common protocol for short-distance communication between chips on the same board.

bq24770 uses SMBus, bq24773 uses I2C.

Think of this as the way the battery controller “talks” to the CPU.0

4. System Instant-on Operation

The device can power the system immediately even if the battery is missing or completely dead.

This is useful for laptops where you want the laptop to turn on with only AC power.

5. Supplement Mode with Synchronous BATFET Control

BATFET → A special MOSFET (transistor) that connects/disconnects the battery safely.

Supplement mode → When the adapter can’t supply enough power, the battery supplements the power to the system.

Synchronous control → Efficient switching to reduce energy loss.

6. Ultra Fast Input Current DPM (100 μs)

DPM = Dynamic Power Management

It means the controller can quickly limit the input current to avoid overloading the power source in 100 microseconds (μs).

Helps prevent tripping fuses or overloading USB adapters.

7. Ultra Low Quiescent Current

Quiescent current = the current the chip uses when idle.

600 µA → Very low, meaning the chip uses very little power when the system is sleeping.

PFM Light Load Efficiency > 80% at 20 mA load

PFM = Pulse Frequency Modulation, a method to save power at low currents.

At 20 mA, it wastes less than 20% of energy — very efficient.
8. High Accuracy Power / Current Monitor

The chip can measure:

IADP = Adapter current (current from AC adapter)

IBAT = Battery current (current going into/out of battery)

PMON = System power

CPU Throttling → The processor can reduce its speed if power is low or overheating. The controller helps with this by monitoring power.

PROCHOT → Processor Hot signal. The controller can signal the CPU to throttle if it’s overheating.

9. Programmable Limits

Charge Voltage → Max voltage for battery (accurate to ±0.5%)

Charge Current → Max charging current (accurate to ±2%)

Input Current Limit → Max current from adapter

Minimum System Voltage Regulation → System won’t drop below a set voltage.

This makes it flexible for different batteries and power sources.


11. High Integration

Drivers built-in for MOSFETs:

NMOS ACFET and RBFET Driver → Switch the adapter current efficiently.

PMOS Battery FET Gate Driver → Connect/disconnect battery safely.

Internal loop compensation → Simplifies circuit design for stable charging.

Automatic trickle charge → Slowly wakes up deeply discharged batteries so the gas gauge can read them.

12. Switching Frequency: 600 kHz to 1.2 MHz

Refers to how fast the internal DC-DC converter switches.

High frequency → smaller inductors/capacitors → smaller circuit size.

Programmable → Can optimize for efficiency or noise.



1. High-efficiency, synchronous, NVDC1 battery charge controller

High-efficiency → It wastes very little power while charging batteries.

Synchronous → Uses both high-side and low-side MOSFETs in a controlled way, which is more efficient than just using one switch.

Think: it’s like having both a gas pedal and brake pedal perfectly coordinated to save fuel.

NVDC1 → Refers to Non-Volatile Direct Current, version 1, meaning it remembers settings even if power is lost.
2. Low component count for space-constrained, multi-chemistry applications

Low component count → The chip has a lot of built-in features, so you need fewer extra parts on the PCB.

Space-constrained → Ideal for small devices like laptops, tablets, or embedded boards.

Multi-chemistry battery charging → Can charge different types of batteries (Li-ion, Li-polymer, etc.) safely.

3. Power Path Management

Power path management → The chip decides how power flows between adapter, battery, and system.

System regulated at battery voltage but does not drop below system minimum voltage (programmable)

Normally, the system voltage follows the battery voltage.

If battery voltage is low, the chip ensures the system voltage stays above a minimum so your device doesn’t crash.

Benefit: Even if the battery is dead or removed, the system still works using adapter power.

4. Supplement Current

Battery supplement current → When the adapter cannot provide enough current for the system, the battery temporarily “helps out.”

Think: if your charger can only give 3A, but your laptop needs 5A, the battery supplies the extra 2A.

Prevents adapter overload → Stops your power source from being pushed too hard.
5. MOSFET Drivers

ACFET = Adapter Control FET → Switches current from the adapter efficiently.

Reverse blocking FET (RBFET) → Prevents current from flowing backward from battery to adapter.

P-channel battery FET driver → Controls connecting/disconnecting battery safely.

High-side and low-side MOSFETs for switching regulator → Needed for DC-DC conversion, i.e., efficiently changing voltage levels to charge battery or power system.

6. Monitoring

IADP → Current from the adapter.

IBAT → Battery charge or discharge current.

PMON → System power (how much power the system is using).

Benefit: The chip constantly monitors currents to prevent overcurrent, overcharging, and system instability.

7. PROCHOT Output

PROCHOT = Processor Hot signal → A programmable output to the CPU.

If the system or battery is stressed (high current or overheating), the chip can signal the CPU to throttle back, i.e., slow down to avoid damage.


